Z||000000||ezsurf||04-20-99||09:55 PM||||<br>I'm considering adding a set of headers to my 2.8l engine.  Can anyone recommend a set that yields reasonable performance, without an astronomic price?
Z||000001||theogre||04-20-99||10:07 PM||||because v6 parts are made in low volume, or totaly custom 1 off parts, they will always be pricey. I don't think I've seen anything under around 250-300$us for fiero headers. A "best option" header set has both sides and replaces, or eliminates, that skinny slagging "Y" pipe. A good option retains the Y.<p>you should also expect to change the ECM PROM for best results. the factory one is calibrated for the stock exaust, depending on what headers you use, they could throw off the ECM. for example a pair of headers, and a full dual exhaust, would likely screw up the ECM. The headers from Fiero Store that retain the factory Y pipe probably won't be a problem to the ECM. They breath better, but not so much so as to freak out the whole engine air flow.
Z||000002||ezsurf||04-20-99||10:14 PM||||Thanks Ogre.
Z||000003||DXR_DAD||04-20-99||11:09 PM||||What`s up ezsurf,<p>I`am thinking the some, but all Iam finding is $350.00+ (Fiero Store sprint) &  West Coast Fiero flow-well), Coast does replace the "Y", $595.00 with ceramic coat. <p>Let me know what you find.
Z||000004||Punkndrublic||04-21-99||05:59 PM||||How bout making some?? People were talking bout doing that with their 4's on another thread. But how feasable is it with the 6. I was thinking of doing my own in a month or two, the only problem I see is the time required to make sure things are lining up right, and of course get a hold of a mandrel pipe bender, how ever much those things cost.
Z||000005||PaUL||04-21-99||06:16 PM||||If anyone on this forum has the means to design and fabricate a set of headers for the 2.8v6, and can produce them in quantity for sale under $200, please let me know. Ive been dreaming about headers for years.<p>Seems like all the ones Ive seen cost over $400. I think they are so expensive because the manufacturers insist on making them out of stainless steel. A mild steel set should cost alot less.
Z||000006||ezsurf||04-21-99||06:40 PM||||DXR_DAD,<br>  I've had my Fiero for about three weeks.  I knew the car needed a fair amount of work to restore it into the condition I want it.  I've been collecting parts ever since I bought it.  I'm planning on pulling the engine this weekend.  I haven't explored all the aftermarket Fiero accessories yet.  I know modifications to the car are popular.  The 2.8l engine used to be common in Camaros.  However, the Camaro doesn't require all of the tight bends that the Fiero does.  I'm not throughly familiar with how well this engine breathes i.e. intake/exhaust.  But, if I'm going to put a set of headers on the engine, I'm going to do it when I have the engine out.  If I find a cracked exhaust manifold, I don't want to replace it with a stock manifold.  The engine seems to run pretty strong, I really don't expect it to be low on compression.  But, I do think I'm leaking oil through the valve seal guides.  I get a puff of blue smoke when I shift gears.  To have the heads worked on won't be too expensive, but the cylinders are another matter.  I'll be surfing the web for a set of headers.  If I find anything better than the Fiero Store I'll let you know.
Z||000007||Ross||04-21-99||09:07 PM||||Hay ez its me again.From what ive hard the 2.8 in the Fiero is suposed to be alittle more responsive than the other variety of GM 2.8s on the road. Can any on back this up?
Z||000008||PaUL||04-22-99||03:12 AM||||hey that could be a good idea. Buy a set of cheap headers for another car with 2.8 V6 and adapt it for the Fiero. Someone just has to figure out the best way, Im sure it can be done. Ive seen headers for X,J, and A cars from Hedman for less than $150. Those cars are all FWD and should be easier to adapt than a RWD F car.
Z||000009||Phil||04-22-99||06:56 PM||||If you are looking for a cheap way of getting headers try modifing your existing V-6 ones.<br>When you remove and look at the stock ones you will see quite a bit of restrictive material where the risers meet the main tube.  Weld up all cracks and have a bead of weld run around the outside where the riser goes into the flange. Lay an exhaust gasket over the ports and remove the excess factory weld from the inside of the flange. You will notice a performance difference for about $25<br>($15 for welding $10 for carbide grinding bits $0 for time)
Z||000010||HF6582||04-23-99||12:41 AM||||i saw a set of headers that FOCOA sells.<br>very nice looking but expensive.<br>@$600
Z||000011||PaUL||04-23-99||01:36 PM||||Ive seen header weld-up kits for V8s from Hooker and Hedman. These allow you to custom build your own set of headers and come with pre bent pipes and exhaust port flanges.<p>Unlike the flanges on the stock fiero exhaust, most header flanges are a one piece design, instead of the 3 separate ones on the fiero. Ive found this to be a problem. It allows the ports to become misaligned. When I take one of my manifolds and place it on a level surface port side down, all the flange surfaces are not flush. This creates gaps between the flange and head causing exhaust leaks. If you could find a generic 1 piece header flange for the 2.8, it wouldnt be too hard for a welder to fabricate the rest of the headers.  
